\Title {Data-Driven Penetration Prediction and Probabilistic Impingement Screening Based on Mie Scattering Imaging}
\Author {Linan Jiang}
\Language {en}
\Copyright {\textcopyright \ 2026. This work is licensed under a Creative Commons "Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike 4.0 International" (BY-NC-SA 4.0) license.}
\CopyrightURL {https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/deed.en}
\Subject {Pilot diesel injections in multi-fuel marine engines are short, highly transient events. If the liquid spray reaches the piston crown or cylinder wall before ignition develops, it can cause wall wetting, lubricant dilution, and poor mixture preparation. This thesis develops an engineering screening workflow that converts high-speed Mie-scattering spray videos into penetration measurements and trained surrogate models for early spray--wall impingement risk assessment. The data consist of room-temperature, non-reacting, top-view recordings from the W\"artsil\"a Optical Spray Combustion Chamber, covering multiple operating conditions and nozzle geometries.  The workflow combines multi-hole video processing, plume-wise feature extraction, and a reduced-order trajectory fit that smooths noisy labels and mitigates the downward bias caused by right-censoring at the camera field-of-view limit. Its two central contributions are a censoring-aware measurement-to-target construction and a time-resolved empirical scaling analysis. On the Level-3 quality-controlled archive, the effective pressure-difference exponent is not constant: across the well-supported 0.3--0.7 ms interval it ranges from approximately 0.45 to 0.69, and then moves towards the classical 0.25 post-breakup value as the surviving sample count and regression support decrease. Conditional on pressure difference, ambient pressure also retains a measurable partial association with penetration. These results motivate, but do not elevate to a universal spray law, the physics-guided scaling used by the surrogate.  The resulting targets are instantiated in a three-stage curriculum that predicts the penetration mean together with a heteroscedastic uncertainty estimate. On 540,367 points from 9 nozzle campaigns under an uncensored evaluation protocol, the final multilayer perceptron (MLP) model achieves a five-seed mean root mean squared error (RMSE) of 4.19 mm and mean absolute error (MAE) of 2.98 mm with conservative 1-sigma/2-sigma uncertainty coverage of 88.8\%/99.0\%, reducing RMSE by more than 54\% relative to calibrated Hiroyasu--Arai and Naber--Siebers baselines. Post-training feature-wise linear modulation (FiLM) adaptation mainly improved probabilistic calibration scores relative to a controlled pooled baseline, in known-family adaptation experiments. A sparse variational Gaussian process (SVGP) alternative attains better point accuracy but markedly less conservative coverage; the two backbones represent different accuracy--coverage trade-offs.  A simplified geometric projection finally maps the predicted penetration distribution onto probabilistic wall-impingement screening scores. The workflow is intended as an early-stage screening tool within its measured operating domain, ahead of---not in place of---computational fluid dynamics (CFD) simulation and engine testing.}
\Keywords {uncertainty quantification\sep surrogate modeling\sep Gaussian process\sep spray imaging\sep penetration\sep Mie scattering}
